-     `MetricUnit(value)` makes one from a `Double`, and `MetricUnit(quantity)` from a quantity
-     (so `MetricUnit(Joule/Second)` is the watt). The companion's `underlying` given marks it as
-     a `Double` underneath.
+     `MetricUnit(value)` crea uno a partir de un `Double`, y `MetricUnit(quantity)` a partir de
+     una magnitud (así, `MetricUnit(Joule/Second)` es el vatio). El given `underlying` del objeto
+     compañero lo marca como un `Double` por debajo.

- entry .Temperature   an absolute temperature, measured from absolute zero, shown on the scale in scope
+ entry .Temperature   una temperatura absoluta, medida desde el cero absoluto, que se muestra en la escala del ámbito

= entry .Temperature   an absolute temperature, measured from absolute zero, shown on the scale in scope
-   detail
+   detail

-       Temperature is measured from an arbitrary zero, so thirty degrees plus thirty degrees is
-       not sixty degrees of anything: it is an *offset* quantity, and `Temperature` — the opaque
-       type `quantitative.internal.Temperature`, a `Double` of kelvins — keeps it apart from
-       the `Quantity[Kelvins[1]]` which measures a *difference* of temperature. A temperature is
-       created on a scale, `Celsius(30)`, `Fahrenheit(86)`, `Kelvin(303.15)` (the last needs
-       `import quantitative.*`, as its `apply` is not on the umbrella) or `Temperature(303.15)`,
-       and shown on whichever `TemperatureScale` is imported from `temperatureScales`:
+       La temperatura se mide desde un cero arbitrario, así que treinta grados más treinta grados
+       no son sesenta grados de nada: es una magnitud de *desplazamiento*, y `Temperature` —el
+       tipo opaco `quantitative.internal.Temperature`, un `Double` de kelvins— la mantiene aparte
+       del `Quantity[Kelvins[1]]` que mide una *diferencia* de temperatura. Una temperatura se
+       crea en una escala, `Celsius(30)`, `Fahrenheit(86)`, `Kelvin(303.15)` (la última necesita
+       `import quantitative.*`, pues su `apply` no está en el paraguas) o `Temperature(303.15)`,
+       y se muestra en la `TemperatureScale` que se importe de `temperatureScales`:

